# NestJS Knowledge

## Modules

- One module per business domain in `src/modules/<domain>`; infrastructure adapters are modules
  too (`DatabaseModule`, `MailModule`, `StorageModule`).
- A module `exports` only what other modules may use — usually its service. Repositories,
  mappers and controllers are never exported.
- `@Global()` only for true infrastructure used everywhere: config, database, the CLS/transaction
  module. Never for a business module.
- Dynamic modules (`forRoot` / `forRootAsync` / `register`) only in `infrastructure` or `config`,
  configured from `AppConfig` via `useFactory` + `inject`.
- No `forwardRef`. A cycle means a boundary is wrong (`01-architecture`).

## Providers and DI

- Constructor injection only, `private readonly`. Never `new` an injectable, never
  `moduleRef.get()` in business code.
- Ports are injected by token. An interface cannot be a token, so pair the interface with an
  UPPER_SNAKE symbol next to it:

```ts
export const STORAGE_PORT = Symbol("STORAGE_PORT");
export interface StoragePort {
  putObject(input: PutObjectInput): Promise<void>;
}

{ inject: [AppConfig], provide: STORAGE_PORT, useFactory: createStorageAdapter }

constructor(@Inject(STORAGE_PORT) private readonly storage: StoragePort) {}
```

- A constructor-injected class must be imported as a **value**, not `import type` — decorator
  metadata needs the runtime reference (`22-imports`).

## Scopes

| Scope | Use |
| --- | --- |
| Default (singleton) | Everything. |
| `REQUEST` | Never in business code. It bubbles up the whole injection chain and re-creates it per request. |
| `TRANSIENT` | Only for a stateless helper that must not be shared (rare). |

Per-request data (current user, locale, request id, active transaction) travels through
`nestjs-cls` (`ClsService`), not request-scoped providers.

## Request pipeline order

```
middleware → guards → interceptors (before) → pipes → handler
           → interceptors (after) → exception filters
```

Global guards run in the order their `APP_GUARD` providers are registered:

```
ThrottlerGuard → CsrfGuard → JwtAuthGuard → PermissionGuard
```

| Piece | Job | Registered as |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Middleware | cookie-parser, helmet, CLS setup, request id | `main.ts` / `ClsModule` |
| Guards | may this request proceed at all (rate, CSRF, authn, role) | `APP_GUARD` |
| Interceptors | request-scoped logging context, `ZodSerializerInterceptor` | `APP_INTERCEPTOR` |
| Pipes | `ZodValidationPipe` | `APP_PIPE` |
| Filters | `AllExceptionsFilter` | `APP_FILTER` |

Register global pipes/guards/interceptors/filters as `APP_*` providers in `AppModule`, not with
`app.useGlobal*()`, so they get DI and the e2e app picks them up automatically.

## Guards: no business logic

A guard answers "authenticated? right role? CSRF valid? under the rate limit?" from the request
and route metadata only. It never loads a domain row to decide ownership — that is the service's
job (`09-auth`). Guards read metadata with `Reflector.getAllAndOverride(KEY, [handler, class])`.

## Custom decorators

Live in `common/decorators`:

```ts
export const IS_PUBLIC_KEY = "isPublic";
export const Public = (): CustomDecorator => SetMetadata(IS_PUBLIC_KEY, true);

export const RequirePermission = (permission: string, action: string): CustomDecorator =>
  SetMetadata(PERMISSION_KEY, { action, permission });

export const CurrentUser = createParamDecorator(
  (_data: unknown, context: ExecutionContext): AuthUser => {
    const request = context.switchToHttp().getRequest<AuthenticatedRequest>();
    return request.user;
  },
);
```

- Compose repeated stacks with `applyDecorators` (`x-error-codes` in `openapi/openapi.yaml`, `@AuthEndpoint`).
- `ExecutionContext` is only touched in guards, interceptors, filters and param decorators. Check
  `context.getType()` if the code could run for non-HTTP contexts (queues, CLI).

## Lifecycle hooks

- `OnModuleInit` — warm-up that must succeed before serving (verify SMTP config, connect a client).
- `OnApplicationBootstrap` — start pollers/workers (outbox dispatcher).
- `OnModuleDestroy` / `BeforeApplicationShutdown` — stop workers, flush, close pools.
- They only fire on SIGTERM when `enableShutdownHooks()` is on.

## Bootstrap

`main.ts` stays tiny; the HTTP setup lives in one `configureApp(app)` function
(`src/app.setup.ts`) that the e2e app factory calls too, so tests run the same stack.

Checklist for `configureApp`:

- [ ] `NestFactory.create<NestExpressApplication>(AppModule, { bufferLogs: true })`, then the
      app logger (`14-logging`)
- [ ] `app.setGlobalPrefix('api', { exclude: HEALTH_ROUTES })` — `/health*` stays outside (`05-api-design`)
- [ ] `app.set("trust proxy", config.app.trustProxy)` — correct client IP for throttling and
      `Secure` cookies behind a proxy
- [ ] `helmet()`
- [ ] `cookieParser()`
- [ ] `app.enableCors({ credentials: true, origin: config.app.corsOrigins, allowedHeaders:
      ["Content-Type", "X-CSRF-Token", "X-Locale", "X-Request-Id"] })` — an explicit allowlist,
      never `origin: true` or `"*"` with credentials
- [ ] no Swagger: `OpenApiController` serves `openapi/openapi.yaml` as-is at `/api/openapi.yaml` and
      `/api/openapi.json` (`05-api-design`)
- [ ] `app.enableShutdownHooks()`
- [ ] body size limit set explicitly (`app.useBodyParser("json", { limit: "1mb" })`)

`AppModule` providers: `APP_PIPE` ZodValidationPipe, `APP_INTERCEPTOR` ZodSerializerInterceptor,
`APP_FILTER` AllExceptionsFilter, the four `APP_GUARD`s in the order above.

## Controllers

```ts
@Controller("users")
export class UsersController {
  constructor(private readonly usersService: UsersService) {}

  @Get(":id")
  @ZodSerializerDto(UserResponseDto)
  async getById(@Param() { id }: IdParamDto, @CurrentUser() user: AuthUser) {
    return this.usersService.getById(user, id);
  }
}
```

- Params are validated by a zod DTO too (`IdParamDto` in `common`), not by Nest's `ParseUUIDPipe`,
  whose error bypasses the `VALIDATION_FAILED` envelope.

- One service call per handler. No `@Res()` unless streaming a file; with `@Res()` Nest's
  interceptors and serializer are bypassed. For cookies use `@Res({ passthrough: true })`.
- `@HttpCode()` for non-default success statuses (204 on DELETE, 200 on action POSTs).

## Don't

- Don't use `REQUEST` scope or inject `REQUEST` into services.
- Don't catch and re-wrap errors in controllers; the filter owns the response.
- Don't put `process.env` in module factories — inject `AppConfig`.
- Don't register the same provider in two modules; import the owning module.
